# `namespaced_id!`

<SymbolMeta />

`namespaced_id!` constructs a concrete Upwell stable-ID value from a string literal. It validates the literal through the target type's `new` constructor and yields that type, so it is appropriate for associated constants such as plugin, protocol, scope, slot, and contribution identities.

<SymbolSignature />

## Grammar

```text
namespaced_id!(<id type>, <identifier literal>)
```

`<id type>` is a Rust type implementing the constructor generated by [`namespaced_id_type!`](./namespaced_id_type), for example `PluginId`, `ProtocolId`, `ScopeId`, `PluginSlotId`, or `ContributionId`. `<identifier literal>` must be a Rust string literal; a variable, constant expression, `concat!`, or formatted value does not match this macro.

```rust
use upwell::{Plugin, PluginId, ScopeId, StaticScope, namespaced_id};

struct AuditPlugin;

impl Plugin for AuditPlugin {
    const ID: PluginId = namespaced_id!(PluginId, "acme/audit");
}

struct Request;

impl StaticScope for Request {
    const ID: ScopeId = namespaced_id!(ScopeId, "acme/request");
    const RANK: u8 = 100;
    const NAME: &'static str = "Request";
}
```

The macro expands to a block expression containing a `const` value. It can therefore be used both where a constant is required and in an ordinary runtime expression; no allocation, parsing, or fallible result remains at the call site after successful compilation.

## Identifier literals

An identifier is a slash-separated ASCII path with at least two non-empty segments:

```text
<namespace>/<local path>
```

The first segment is the owning namespace. Everything after the first slash is the local path; it may itself contain more slash-separated segments. Each segment must start with a lowercase ASCII letter or digit. Its remaining characters may be lowercase ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `_`, or `-`.

| Valid | Why |
| --- | --- |
| `"acme/audit"` | namespace and local name |
| `"acme/rpc.request-v2"` | permitted punctuation after a segment start |
| `"acme/platform/connection"` | nested local path |
| `"7g/network"` | a digit may begin a segment |

| Rejected | Reason |
| --- | --- |
| `"audit"` | no namespace separator |
| `"acme/"` or `"acme//audit"` | an empty segment |
| `"Acme/audit"` or `"acme/_audit"` | a segment starts with an unsupported byte |
| `"acme/routér"` or `"acme/audit name"` | non-ASCII or unsupported character |

## Output and diagnostics

The resulting value is exactly the requested ID type. That type exposes `as_str`, `namespace`, `name`, `local_path`, and `parts`; it implements `Display`, `AsRef<str>`, ordering, equality, hashing, and `NamespacedIdType` for namespace comparisons.

Invalid literals fail compilation through the macro's constant evaluation path. The diagnostic includes `invalid namespaced identifier literal: <literal>`. Use `Type::new("...")` instead when input must be handled as a fallible `Result` rather than rejected at compile time.

The macro validates format only. Category safety comes from the target type: a `PluginId` cannot be substituted for a `ScopeId`, even when both contain the same text.

## Extension authors

Use a stable, package-owned namespace for externally implemented plugins and protocols, such as `"acme/audit"` or `"example/transport/http"`. Treat the literal as a durable integration identity, not a display label: changing it changes composition, provenance, diagnostics, and tooling references.

Third-party extensions must not use the reserved `upwell/` namespace for plugin contributions. The application validates that policy when it freezes a plugin plan. Framework crates may use that namespace for their own stable IDs.

Use a `ContributionId` for each emitted contribution, rather than reusing a component or provider payload ID. A contribution identity names one plugin emission and must be unique within that plugin.
